statement:
For every integer \(k\ge2\), put \(n=k+1\) and define

\[
\begin{aligned}
\varepsilon_k
&=\frac{k^k}{k^k+(k^3-k-2)(k^2+1)^{k-1}},
&t_k&=\log(1/\varepsilon_k),\\
P_k&=[-1,2]^k\times[0,k-1],
&D_k&=P_k\cap\mathbb Z^{k+1}
=\{-1,0,1,2\}^k\times\{0,\ldots,k-1\}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Writing a lattice index as \(\mathbf I=(\mathbf u,s)\), let its original weight be

\[
w_{(\mathbf u,s)}=
\begin{cases}
1,&\mathbf u\in\{0,1\}^k,\quad s\in\{0,\ldots,k-1\},\\
\varepsilon_k,&\mathbf u\in\{-1,0,1,2\}^k\setminus\{0,1\}^k,
\quad s\in\{0,\ldots,k-1\},\\
0,&(\mathbf u,s)\notin D_k.
\end{cases}
\]

This is a complete globally extendable finite log-concave table. Its maximum weight is \(w_{\max}=1\), and its canonical potential \(\phi_{\mathbf I}=\log(w_{\max}/w_{\mathbf I})\) is zero on the Boolean rows and \(t_k\) on every other positive site. Set \(T_k=\varepsilon_k(4^k-2^k)\), the total weight outside the Boolean cube in one interval slice. The original total mass is \(W_k=k(2^k+T_k)\).

For each target \(\mathbf J=(\mathbf j,\ell)\in D_k\), let \(F_{\mathbf J}\) be the minimal face of \(P_k\) containing \(\mathbf J\). Define \(p^{\mathbf J}\) to be the positive Gibbs tilt of the original weights on all of \(F_{\mathbf J}\cap\mathbb Z^{k+1}\), with mean \(\mathbf J\), using the whole intrinsic lattice of that face. Define

\[
\begin{aligned}
g_{\mathbf J}
&=\sum_{\mathbf I\in F_{\mathbf J}\cap\mathbb Z^{k+1}}
p^{\mathbf J}_{\mathbf I}\phi_{\mathbf I}-\phi_{\mathbf J},\\
\tau_{\mathbf J}
&=\inf_{\substack{H\subseteq\mathbb R^{k+1}\text{ a closed halfspace}\\
\mathbf J\in H}}
\sum_{\mathbf I\in D_k\cap H}w_{\mathbf I}.
\end{aligned}
\]

The centered law exists and is unique as a probability law at every target. Its energy is exactly

\[
\begin{aligned}
G_k
&=t_k\frac{k^2+1}{(k-1)(k+1)^2}
\left[1-\left(\frac{k}{k^2+1}\right)^k\right],\\
g_{(\mathbf j,\ell)}
&=\begin{cases}
G_k,&\mathbf j\in\{0,1\}^k,\\
0,&\mathbf j\notin\{0,1\}^k.
\end{cases}
\end{aligned}
\]

All atom weights and all marginal depths in the following bound are those of the original table:

\[
\begin{aligned}
&\frac{2^{k+1}}{W_k}
\max_{\mathbf J\in D_k}
\max\left\{w_{\mathbf J},\frac{\tau_{\mathbf J}}{1+g_{\mathbf J}}\right\}\\
&\qquad\le
\max\left\{
\frac2k,
\frac{2(1+T_k)}{1+G_k},
2\varepsilon_k4^{k-1}
\right\}\\
&\qquad\le
\max\left\{
\frac2k,
\frac{2[1+(4/k)^k]}{1+(\log k)/3},
\frac12(4/k)^k
\right\}
\longrightarrow0.
\end{aligned}
\]

Consequently no absolute positive \(c\) satisfies the registered tradeoff for every complete globally extendable finite table.
